==================================================
4. WHAT HAS BEEN CONFIRMED
==========================

OpenAI officially supports the OpenAI API.

ChatGPT consumer subscriptions and OpenAI API usage are separate products/services.

OpenAI also provides "Sign in with ChatGPT" for supported external applications, but this appears to primarily provide identity/account-linking functionality rather than general access to the user's ChatGPT consumer environment.

OpenAI's Codex products support ChatGPT-account authentication, but this does not automatically establish that unrelated third-party applications can reuse that authentication mechanism.

OpenAI does not currently document a general-purpose third-party API that allows arbitrary desktop applications to access:

* normal ChatGPT conversations
* ChatGPT history
* ChatGPT Projects
* ChatGPT Memory
* Custom GPTs
* ChatGPT files
* the complete ChatGPT consumer experience

Therefore, the desired consumer-account integration remains unresolved / unavailable through a clearly documented general third-party interface.

==================================================
5. IMPORTANT DISTINCTION
========================

There are three separate concepts:

A. IDENTITY

"Who is this user?"

This can potentially be solved through an identity/OAuth system.

B. AUTHORIZATION

"Has the user explicitly permitted AI-Hub to access something?"

This requires an officially supported authorization mechanism and defined permissions/scopes.

C. CHATGPT SERVICE ACCESS

"Can AI-Hub use this user's ChatGPT subscription to send messages, access models, conversations, history, files, projects, memory, etc.?"

This requires OpenAI to expose and authorize those capabilities to third-party applications.

Having A does NOT automatically provide B or C.

Having B does NOT automatically provide C.

==================================================
6. CURRENT CLASSIFICATION
=========================

For the desired feature:

"User signs into their normal ChatGPT account and uses the consumer ChatGPT service inside AI-Hub without an API key."

Current status:

🔴 NOT CURRENTLY CONFIRMED AS AN OFFICIALLY SUPPORTED GENERAL THIRD-PARTY INTEGRATION.

This classification must be revisited if OpenAI publishes a suitable third-party authorization/API mechanism or explicitly approves AI-Hub.

==================================================
7. EXISTING UNOFFICIAL CHATGPT DESKTOP CLIENT
=============================================

The developer project currently used on Parrot OS:

ChatGPT Desktop
Created by Joshua Redmond

is explicitly described as an unofficial desktop application/wrapper.

It demonstrates that a standalone Linux desktop application can provide a ChatGPT experience without requiring the user to manually provide an API key.

However, technical functionality of an unofficial application does NOT prove that the same architecture is officially supported for a new independent application.

AI-Hub may study the project's architecture for educational purposes, but must not blindly copy or reproduce undocumented authentication mechanisms.

AI-Hub must remain within provider-approved mechanisms.

==================================================
8. AI-HUB ARCHITECTURAL DECISION
================================

AI-Hub must NOT be redesigned around a universal authentication mechanism.

Each provider adapter will support whatever officially permitted mechanism is available for that provider.

Example:

ChatGPTAdapter
→ OpenAI-approved authentication mechanism, if available
→ otherwise official OpenAI API authentication

GeminiAdapter
→ Google-approved mechanism

ClaudeAdapter
→ Anthropic-approved mechanism

GrokAdapter
→ xAI-approved mechanism

PerplexityAdapter
→ Perplexity-approved mechanism

KimiAdapter
→ Kimi-approved mechanism

The ProviderManager must remain provider-agnostic.

